What Is It?
~~~~~~~~~~~
    Used in conjunction with the AmigaDOS Search command, this file gives
you the powerful ability of searching through the `GU40_Longs' file for the
values you're interested in. Being as the actual listing is huge, this file
makes the job MUCH more easy!

How to Install?
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
    To install this file, you'll need to copy the "GetUserSearch" file, as
well as this README file somwehere on your system. I chose to keep my copy
inside the "GU40Pack/" directory, and then stored this directory in my TEXTS:
assigned path(this is what I call my gfiles area).

    In your BBSMENU file, which is located in your CNET: path, add this NEW
line to the bottom of Menu # 1; Maintenance Menu:

        GUS, Get User Search | {#0<path>GetUserSearch}
                               |  ~~~|~~
        Control-Q character ---'     `--> Where you stored the file above.

    In your "Shell-Startup" file, which is located in your S: directory,
add the following line to the BOTTOM of the file:

        Alias gu "search <path>gu40_longs [] PATTERN"

    Again, replace <path> above with the directory you stored the GU40_Longs
file. Also, everything AFTER the gu bove should be enclosed in double quotes
as shown.

    Now SAVE both files, then reload your BBSTEXT file by clicking your
Control Panel once, then pressing RAMIGA-T to reload texts.

Using the GUS and GU Commands
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
    Enter your bbs, then type "GUS ?" (minus quotes) from any command prompt
to display the help page for Searcher. Hopefully, this page will give you
all the help you need in using this file.

    The "Alias" line you added to your "Shell-Startup" file gives you a GU
command you can use directly in Amiga Dos. Open a shell window, then type:

        1> gu <text>

    Where <text> is the text you wish to search for. Line numbers will ALWAYS
be shown when using the GU command, and you'll always use the standard text
display format. Remember, though, that if your text contains SPACES, please
enclose them in double quotes, as shown below:

        1> gu "total calls"

    Lastly, pattern matching is enabled when the GU command is used. Remember
this if using ? or #? in your search text, as they'll be considered wildcards,
instead of actually SEARCHING for a "?" or "#?".

    This file received a complete rewrite, and no longer needs a separate
data file. This will allow you to make updates and/or changes to your list
and still be able to search out your needed values with the utmost of ease!

    If you have ANY problems, please let me know about them ASAP. This file
should work equally well on 3.05c systems and 4.26+ systems, however is NOT
compatible with 2.63 systems.

